<Project>

  <!-- Chain to the repository-wide props. MSBuild stops searching upward at the FIRST
       Directory.Build.props it finds, so without this import every project under tests/ would
       silently lose TreatWarningsAsErrors, MinVer and the Sonar analyzer. Measured, and the
       analyzer is not demoted but ABSENT: without the import its PackageReference is gone from
       the project, so no Sonar rule fires under tests/ at any severity and the build is green
       because nothing looked. Take that measurement on a RESTORING build; one that skips the restore
       keeps the analyzer in stale assets and shows a mere demotion, which is what an earlier
       version of this comment claimed. Three of the four losses were driven and each one moves:
       TreatWarningsAsErrors true to false, MinVerMinimumMajorMinor to empty with its package
       reference gone, and the Sonar reference from three occurrences to none. The fourth, the
       NuGet audit pins, restates the SDK's own defaults today, so NuGetAuditMode reads the same
       with the import and without it and nothing here can show it doing any work. -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove($(MSBuildThisFile), $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..))" />

  <!-- The compiler resolves a <see cref="..."/> only while it is generating the documentation
       file. Without this, a cref naming a member that exists nowhere compiles clean, so a comment
       pointing at a deleted or renamed member reads as a live pointer and the explanation behind
       it is unreachable. CS1591 is suppressed because documentation is not wanted on test members;
       only the resolution is.

       This lives here rather than in each project so a test project added later inherits it. A
       per-project copy is an enumeration, and a new project escapes an enumeration silently. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
